# Round XXIII Track β — POSITIVE CONTROL: parenthesized `for x in (&coll):`.
# Currently blocked by the parser's `looks_like_closure()` heuristic (which
# reads `(v):` as a closure-param position); the un-parenthesized
# `for x in &coll:` is the practical spelling. This control instead pins the
# comprehension iterable form `[x for x in &a]` — the same underlying strip
# preamble (`check_iterable_maybe_amp`) covers it — because the comprehension
# parser DOES land `&a` as `Expr::MutableBorrow` on the iterable (unlike
# stmt-for, which the parser's `parse_ownership_modifier` consumes into the
# `Stmt::For.ownership` field before the iterable is parsed).
#
# Sibling live-corpus regression: `cow_comprehension_amp_source.gg` (whose
# `[x * 2 for x in &a]` shape would false-reject without the strip).
# Core #12 axis-completeness: this is one of the 3 positive controls;
# `pos_for_amp_enumerate_paren.gg` covers `.enumerate()` receiver-wrap;
# `pos_call_arg_amp_unchanged.gg` covers the CallArg strip.

void main():
    Vector[int] a = [1, 2, 3]
    Vector[int] b = [x * 2 for x in &a]
    print(b.len())
    print(b.get(0).unwrap())
    print(b.get(1).unwrap())
    print(b.get(2).unwrap())
